[h1]Platform Efficiency Overlay[/h1]
See if your factory is actually moving, and where it is stuck - no belt readers required.

[h2]The overlay[/h2]
Every machine, belt, space belt and space pipe is coloured by how much of its capacity it is using.
A green overlay means it is keeping up. Red or orange indicates less than 50% throughput is being achieved.

A green dot marks anything that is stuck: goods are sitting on it and nothing is leaving.
That tells you whether something slow is being starved, or whether it is the thing holding everything else up.

[h2]Efficiency over time[/h2]
Select a machine or a platform to see a chart of how much of its capacity it has been using, over the last minute, 5 minutes, 30 minutes, hour or 6 hours.
Hover a bar for the exact figure and how long ago it was. The corner shows the current rate, along with the average and peak across the window.

History is only kept for machines and platforms - belts would multiply the cost several times over, and tell you nothing you cannot read off the machines they feed.
It comes to roughly 35 MB on a finished factory, and none of it is written to your save. Turn it off with [b]peo.history-enabled false[/b] in the debug console if you would rather not have it.

[h2]Platform and space belt totals[/h2]
Select a platform for what it is shipping against what its ports could carry, with its chart underneath.
Space belts and space pipes get the game's own efficiency gauge, measuring the whole bundle rather than one lane of it.

[h2]Using it[/h2]
[list]
[*]Toggle it with the [b]Platform Efficiency[/b] button in the visualization bar, bottom right. It remembers whether it was on.
[*]Nothing is written to your save, so you can remove it at any time.
[*]Requires Shapez Shifter.
[/list]

Source, readme and issue tracker: https://github.com/QuinnBast/Shapez2-Space-Platform-Efficiencies
